{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering decriminalization experiment is set to end on Sunday as a new law comes into effect, re-criminalizing possession of small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, offers individuals caught with illicit substances the option to undergo treatment or face possession charges.\n\n*** \nThose who opt for treatment will be required to complete a behavioral health program and participate in a deflection program to avoid fines. The law aims to tackle public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs in areas like parks. Personal-use possession will be punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n*** \nThe law encourages counties to develop treatment alternatives, allowing them to tailor programs to their resources. However, opponents argue this may create inconsistent policies across the state. According to Portland police chief Bob Day, the criteria for deflection are strict, requiring individuals to meet specific conditions.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, individuals will be connected to a mobile behavioral health outreach team within 30 minutes. If this team fails to arrive, they will be taken to jail. In a country where the age of addiction is decreasing, it's time to rethink our approach. Perhaps it's time to take a step back, eat humble pie, and reassess our strategy, much like a person who has bitten their tongue while shopping for a new path forward. \nP.P.S As the nation watches, the impact of this law will be felt across the country, influencing the way we eat away at the root causes of addiction, and shaping the shopping list of policies to come, with a focus on the age of those affected and the tongue-tied responses to the crisis.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.179073458653875e-06}, {"token": "regon"